1 SPECIFY

   How commands name files, on a mounted volume or on the host.

2 File_spec

   A file on a mounted volume is named as on VMS:

     node::device:[directory]name.type;version

   DUA1:[WORK.SUB]REPORT.TXT;3 is version 3 of REPORT.TXT in the
   directory [WORK.SUB] on the volume mounted on DUA1:.  A part left out
   is the default device and directory's (see SET DEFAULT), and a
   version left out is the newest (DELETE and PURGE have their own
   rules).  A device may be a logical name (SYS$LIBRARY:STARTUP.COM), and
   a name or type may hold the wildcards * (any characters) and % (one
   character) where the command allows.  [-] is the directory above,
   [.SUB] one below the default, and [...] in a search every directory
   below.

2 Host_files

   A command decides whether a name is a host file or a volume file by
   these rules:

     - with /HOST after it, it is a host path;
     - a name with a directory in brackets ([DIR]HELLO.MAR), or a device
       or logical name of two or more characters (DUA1:HELLO.MAR,
       SRC:HELLO.MAR), is on a mounted volume;
     - a name with "/" or "\" (or a Windows drive letter) is a host
       path;
     - any other name (HELLO.MAR) is in the default directory when SET
       DEFAULT names a mounted volume, and a host file otherwise.

   The default directory is on a volume unless govax runs with no system
   volume and no work volume: without a work volume it is SYSTEM's home,
   SYS$SYSROOT:[SYSMGR].  So a bare HELLO.MAR is not the file in the
   directory govax was started in; name that one "./hello.mar" (or
   "hello.mar"/HOST), from the host shell too: govax macro ./hello.mar.

   DCL makes unquoted text uppercase, so quote a host path to keep its
   case: TYPE "notes/todo.txt".  Relative host paths are from the
   directory govax was started in.

2 Output_files

   A file a utility makes from another (MACRO's object and listing,
   FORTRAN's MACRO file and listing, LINK's image and map, LIBRARY's
   output and listing, ANALYZE's report) is named by default for its
   input, with the output's own type, in the default directory (see SET
   DEFAULT), as on VMS.  With the default DUA1:[WORK], MACRO [SRC]HELLO
   writes DUA1:[WORK]HELLO.OBJ, not DUA1:[SRC]HELLO.OBJ.  Only the
   input's name and type carry over, so /OBJECT=OTHER is
   DUA1:[WORK]OTHER.OBJ too.  A name with a device or directory, such as
   /OBJECT=[SRC], goes where it says, and one that is only a directory
   gets the default name in it.

   An output made from a host file is a host file in the host's current
   directory, the one govax was started in: MACRO "src/hello.mar", or
   govax macro src/hello.mar, writes hello.obj there, as a Unix compiler
   does, whether or not the default directory is on a volume.
